{"type": "thinking", "text": "Gel kept its own 0.1 / 80. So switching Tablet→Gel does not carry over 3; it restores gel's previously entered values. This is \"per-form memory\", which arguably is different from \"carry over\".\n\nHmm. Maybe the \"carry over\" is about the amount fields when the Preparation type changes (Container vs Sachet for Gel; Total dose vs Release rate for Patch). But the note says \"Preparation form picker ... when the form is changed.\"\n\nLet me think about what the bug being fixed likely was. Perhaps: before the fix, switching forms would clear the amount; now the amount from the previous form is carried into the new form. That's what I tested and it doesn't happen. But there's also the possibility that the fix was about multiple amount fields in one form: \"every amount field is now carried over\" — e.g., the gel has 2 amount fields; when changing form, the values of all amount fields (not just the first) should be carried forward.\n\nHmm, an alternative interpretation: The amount fields in the same form persist across form changes (i.e., the amounts you typed are remembered). We verified they're remembered when returning to same form, but not carried across.\n\nLet me test a cleaner scenario to decide: start fresh (reopen the add-medicine sheet), enter Tablet strength 2, switch to Patch, check \"Total amount in patch (mg)\". If empty → cross-form carry-over missing. That's likely the bug. Actually I already effectively tested this: Tablet 3 → Patch was empty (Patch was opened after gel though; patch's own field was empty).\n\nLet me do a definitive test: While on Gel with 0.1/80, switch to Patch → check patch field. Then set patch field to 0.72, switch to Tablet → check tablet strength. This tests carry-over in both directions. We already know patch was empty after gel. So no carry from gel to patch.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe carry-over only applies when field name matches? Tablet strength (mg) vs Vial strength (mg) vs Total amount in patch (mg) - names differ. Gel concentration (%) vs Total gel amount (g). None match. So if implementation carries only when field id matches... but then what would carry over between different forms? Nothing. So that can't be the intent.\n\nPerhaps the intent: when switching form, values should carry over positionally — first amount field → first amount field, second → second, etc. The fix says \"every amount field is now carried over\" implying previously maybe only one was. So for Gel→Patch, patch's one field should get gel's first amount (0.1). It's empty. Bug.\n\nBut maybe I'm on the wrong track: perhaps \"amount fields\" refers to the quick preset buttons? No.\n\nLet me look at the plan: also \"adding medicines: categories and preparation forms\" is a target area. So testing all categories/forms is expected. The carry-over is a changed feature to verify.\n\nI need to be careful: reporting a bug that isn't real is also bad. Let me examine more carefully. Maybe the carry-over happens only when the new form's amount field is empty? In the Tablet→Injection case, Injection's field was empty, and it did not get 3. So no.\n\nAlternatively, maybe carry-over occurs only after saving? i.e., you save a medicine of one form, then add another medicine and it prefills? Unlikely.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider: Maybe the amount state is stored per-field-ID but the same field IDs are reused across forms in the implementation; and switching forms with a display of a different field means the value silently persists in the hidden state. When you first switch to Injection, the \"Vial strength\" field was empty because it's a different field ID. So the \"carry over\" change may be about the `TextField` value being reset when switching — and the actual fix might be that the amount fields are preserved per-form (as observed). The phrase \"every amount field is now carried over when the form is changed\" could mean the draft values carry over across the form change (i.e., when you switch away and back, they're kept). We observed that per-form values ARE retained. Hmm, but that's \"retained\", not \"carried over\".\n\nWait, but maybe on this build the fix is present and working. The task says \"Changed in this build\" — these are areas to verify, not necessarily bugged. The task is to test and find remaining bugs. So maybe the carry-over works in the intended way and my test interpretation is wrong.\n\nLet me test the actual visible behavior more: What does \"carried over\" mean to a user? When I switch form, the amount from the old form should show in the new form. It didn't. That's the most natural reading. I'm fairly confident that's a bug.\n\nBut before reporting, let me check the group editor and Home cards too, and gather all bugs. Also, maybe there's a different carry-over: within the form, the \"Preparation type\" toggle (Ampule/Vial, Container/Sachet, Total dose/Release rate). Let's test that: In Injection form with Vial strength 10, switch Vial→Ampule, check amount retained. That's \"preparation type\", not form.\n\nLet me focus. First, finish exploring the forms and categories. Let me set patch Total amount to something and check.\n\nActually, let me now test the carry-over from Patch's \"Total dose\" to \"Release rate\" — both are patch specification modes with different amount fields. When changing \"Patch specification\" from Total dose to Release rate, the amount field changes from \"Total amount in patch (mg)\" to something like \"Release rate (µg/day)\". Does the amount carry over? That's a \"preparation type\" switch.\n\nLet's explore patch: tap Patch."}
