{"type": "thinking", "text": "Whoa. Now switching Injection→Gel, the Gel concentration and Total gel amount are BOTH EMPTY. Previously when I returned to Gel from Patch, they were 0.1/80. So switching from a different form cleared the Gel values? That contradicts \"carry over\". Interesting!\n\nWait, so maybe the carry-over is directional: when the form changes, the new form's amount fields are set from the old form's amount values, and if the old form's fields don't map, they're cleared. Here Injection had \"10\" in Vial strength. Gel has two amount fields. Neither got 10, both empty. So no carry.\n\nBut when Injection→Gel, the previously retained 0.1/80 were cleared. So each form's amounts are NOT retained independently; they get overwritten on form change by the old form's values via some mapping. The mapping for Injection→Gel failed to produce values (both empty).\n\nEarlier, Tablet→Capsule carried 50 (mapping strength mg → strength mg works). Tablet→Injection failed (both strength mg but maybe field keys differ). Gel→Gel return kept values because switching Patch→Gel... wait, no. Let me re-check: Sequence was Gel(0.1,80) → Patch (empty) → Gel: 0.1/80 present. If the form change copies old form's amount into new form, then Patch→Gel would have copied Patch's empty amount into gel fields, clearing them. But they were NOT cleared. Hmm.\n\nUnless the copy only happens where the target field's semantic matches and empty otherwise? Let me re-trace carefully:\n\n1. Started Add medicine (Estradiol/Tablet). Typed 3 in Tablet strength.\n2. Switch Tablet→Injection: Vial strength empty. (Old tablet = 3, target strength mg = empty!)\n3. Switch Injection→Gel: both fields empty.\n4. Typed 0.1 in Gel concentration, 80 in Total gel amount.\n5. Switch Gel→Patch: Total amount empty.\n6. Switch Patch→Gel: 0.1 and 80 restored!\n7. Switch Gel→Tablet: 3 restored.\n8. Switch Tablet→Injection: still empty (from step 2? Injection held nothing).\n9. Switch Injection→Tablet: 3 restored.\n10. Tablet→Gel: 0.1/80 restored.\n11. Gel→Patch: patch... wait I tapped Patch at that point? Let me re-trace: After step 10, \"tap 13\" which was Patch? At that point, elements: 13 Patch. Yes, tapped Patch → observed empty... Wait no, in the sequence after step 10 (Gel values 0.1/80), I tapped element 13 (Patch) and got the Patch screen with Total amount empty. Hmm, I didn't verify whether patch had 0.72 previously — I had not entered patch value then.\n\nThen I clicked preset 0.72 in patch → Total amount 0.72. Then switched Patch spec Total dose→Release rate: release rate empty.\n\nThen tapped Antiandrogen category: Tablet strength empty (50 not present).\nThen typed 50, switched to Capsule: 50 carried.\nThen switched category Estradiol: Tablet strength empty (3 not present).\nThen typed 3, switch to Injection: empty.\nThen typed 10 in Injection Vial strength, then switch Injection→Gel: Gel 0.1/80 GONE (empty).\n\nHmm. So earlier Patch→Gel restored gel's values but Injection→Gel cleared them. Why the difference? Maybe because in step 6, switching from Patch (whose amount fields are \"Total amount in patch mg\" / \"release rate\") to Gel... The copy might map field index: patch's total amount index 0 → gel concentration index 0 (empty), patch release rate index 1 → gel total amount index 1 (empty) → gel should be empty. But it wasn't. So no copy happened then.\n\nMaybe the difference is that in step 6 the gel values were \"remembered\" because form state was per-form and persisted... but then Injection→Gel cleared it. Hmm, unless entry via keyboard without navigating away? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe I misremember step 6. Let me look at the observation after step 6: Gel concentration \"0.1\", Total gel amount \"80\". Yes.\n\nWhat's different about step 13 (Injection→Gel)? In step 13, I had just typed 10 into Vial strength while the keyboard was open and then tapped Gel directly (keyboard open). In step 6, I had pressed BACK to dismiss keyboard, then switched... Actually, in step 6, I tapped Patch (keyboard dismissed), then tapped Gel. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the behavior is about an amount \"carry over\" buffer: when a field's text changes, it updates a shared amount buffer; when form changes, the target fields are populated from the buffer. If buffer holds multiple values... Hmm, complex.\n\nAlternatively maybe the behavior is: form switch copies the current form's amount into the new form's amount only if both have the same number of amount fields? Let's check:\n- Tablet (1 field) → Capsule (1 field): carried 50. ✓\n- Tablet (1) → Injection (1): did NOT carry 3. ✗\n- Injection (1) → Gel (2): did not carry, cleared. \n- Gel (2) → Patch (1): did not carry; patch empty (but patch was already empty).\n- Patch (1) → Gel (2): gel values kept as 0.1/80?? That contradicts copy. Unless the copy from Patch's fields was empty and the gel's existing values were retained because... hmm, no.\n\nWait — maybe I got the element numbering wrong in step 6. At that time, after step 5 (Gel→Patch), the Patch screen had element 12 = Gel. Then I tapped element 12 and got Gel with 0.1/80. That seems right.\n\nBut hold on, maybe in step 5 I tapped Patch from Gel, and then in step 6 tapped Gel — the state might be per-form remembered. Then in step 13 Injection→Gel, why cleared? Because the amount buffer of Injection (single field) got applied to Gel, clearing its two fields? Perhaps the mapping is by field index AND field \"kind\": Injection's field kind = \"strength\" (mg). Gel's fields kind = \"concentration\" and \"amount\". No match, so no copy... but then why cleared?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the app keeps per-(form) drafts but when you type in a field, it mirrors the text into a shared \"amount\" variable that is used to prefill ALL amount fields when displayed. When switching to Gel, the Gel fields are initialized from the shared variable(s). Hmm.\n\nLet me consider a possibly buggy implementation the QA should catch: \"every amount field is now carried over when the form is changed\" — maybe on this build, the carry-over is buggy: when switching to a form with more fields, the second field is cleared, or values are duplicated, etc.\n\nLet me do a more controlled experiment. Start a fresh Add medicine (cancel and reopen) to reset. Then systematically:\n\nCase A: Tablet (1 field) strength=7 → Capsule (1 field). Expect 7? We saw carry with 50.\nCase B: Capsule with 7 → back to Tablet: does Tablet get 7? (or its own previous?)\nCase C: Tablet 7 → Gel (2 fields): expect both gel fields = 7? or clear?\nCase D: Gel (conc=0.3, amount=80) → Tablet: expect Tablet=0.3? \nCase E: Gel → Patch: expect patch=0.3? or 80?\n\nThe expected behavior per changelog is ambiguous, but likely each new form's amount fields take the \"last entered amount\". Let me test.\n\nActually, let's think about what makes a clean, testable claim: \"When the preparation form is changed, amount fields are carried over.\" The most defensible bug: Enter amount in form A, switch to form B, the amount field is empty. That's what we saw Tablet→Injection. But is it \"empty because it's a different field\"? The changelog says every amount field is carried over, so the value should be there.\n\nHmm, but if I report and the dev says \"carry over only applies when returning to a previously used form\" then it's rejected — fine, but we lose nothing except steps.\n\nActually hold on. Maybe I should test what \"carried over\" means with an intentional scenario: Enter Tablet strength 7, switch to Capsule (1 field) — value 7 appears. Now switch Capsule→Tablet — does Tablet show 7 (carried over) or its own 7 (already 7)? Both 7, ambiguous. Let me use Capsule 50 → Tablet to see if Tablet becomes 50. In my earlier sequence, after Capsule (50) I tapped Estradiol category and Tablet was empty. That's category change, which may clear.\n\nLet me test Capsule 50 → Tablet (same category Antiandrogen). That will tell if carry-over is bidirectional.\n\nI'll do a fresh session. Let me tap Cancel to reset the Add medicine sheet. Actually cancel discards. Then reopen.\n\nWait, but I want to preserve data? Preconditions: fresh install, no medications. I haven't saved anything. Good.\n\nLet me cancel and reopen."}
