{"type": "thinking", "text": "Custom only has Tablet and Capsule. OK.\n\nNow let me look at prep form changes with the custom unit selection: If I choose unit \"μg\" while in Tablet, does the field label change? Also, does the amount carry over when switching unit? Not \"form\" though.\n\nLet me now switch to Estradiol and carefully test the carry-over with two values: tablet → gel, and determine if the fields get values. I already did: Tablet 7 → Gel empty. Hmm.\n\nBut wait — maybe the carry-over works only for forms having the SAME count of amount fields and same labels? Let me test from Gel (2 fields) to something with 2 fields... Estradiol forms with multiple amount fields? Injection has 1. Patch has 1 (or 1 for each spec). So no pair with 2.\n\nHmm, hold on. Maybe I misunderstood the changelog. Let me re-read: \"Preparation form picker: every amount field is now carried over when the form is changed.\"\n\nCould \"Preparation form picker\" refer to the sub-picker \"Preparation type\" (e.g., Container/Sachet, Ampule/Vial, Total dose/Release rate)? No, that's called \"Preparation type\" in the UI, not \"Preparation form\". So the picker with Tablet/Injection/Gel/Patch is \"Preparation form\".\n\n\"when the form is changed\" = when you pick a different form. \"every amount field is now carried over\" = the values in amount fields are transferred to the new form's amount fields.\n\nObserved: Tablet→Gel: gel amount fields empty (expected: 7 in both? or at least one). Tablet→Injection: empty. So bug.\n\nBut Tablet→Capsule carried. That may be because both forms share the exact same field (strength). So maybe the actual implementation copies only fields whose \"id\" matches; the changelog's claim \"every amount field\" suggests they fixed it to copy all amount fields regardless of id? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, the bug could be that the carry-over only works for one amount field (the first) — e.g., Gel's second field (total gel amount) doesn't carry. Let me test: from a form with 1 field into Gel, which field gets the value? We saw neither got 7. So not even the first.\n\nWait wait. Maybe I have the direction wrong. Maybe \"carried over\" means the value from the *previous* form is stored and when you switch to a form that has the same *number of amount fields*, it maps. Hmm.\n\nLet me try a different diagnostic: use the preset buttons. Enter a preset in Tablet (2 mg), switch to Injection: empty. Then enter preset 5 mg in Injection, switch to Tablet: 2 or 5? Let me test. This eliminates typing issues.\n\nActually, let me look at this from a different angle. Perhaps the app's behavior is intended: the amount is remembered *per form* (so switching back retains), and the changelog fix was about keeping the values when switching (before, they were cleared). The phrase \"carried over\" might loosely mean \"kept\". In this build, switching away and back retains values for Gel (0.1/80) — that's \"carried over\" across a form change and back. But Injection→Gel cleared Gel's retained values... Hmm, that contradicts the \"per-form retention\" interpretation.\n\nWait, why did Injection→Gel clear Gel's 0.1/80? Let me re-examine. Actually, in that sequence, I had typed \"10\" in Injection and immediately tapped \"Gel\" with the keyboard open. Maybe that tap caused the app to rebuild the form with the current shared amount state and clear. Let me reproduce: Set Gel values, dismiss keyboard, go to Injection, set 10, dismiss keyboard, then tap Gel. Check gel values.\n\nHmm, I did have the keyboard open in that case (element 22 tap after typing). So the keyboard-open factor might matter! Let me test both.\n\nActually, in my earlier sequence step 6 (Patch→Gel), the keyboard was dismissed before switching. In the Injection→Gel step, keyboard was open. So maybe when keyboard is open, switching form does something different (loses state).\n\nThat's a plausible bug: switching form while the numeric keyboard is open fails to carry over the amount, while switching with keyboard dismissed works. But Tablet→Capsule carried 50 after keyboard dismissed. Tablet→Injection (keyboard dismissed) failed though. So still inconsistent.\n\nHmm hold on, let me re-examine the Tablet→Injection failure. Maybe it's because the injection form has the sub-type \"Ampule\" default, and the amount field is labeled \"Vial strength (mg)\" — maybe when Ampule is selected, the amount belongs to Ampule but the label is wrong? And when I switch to Vial, the value might appear? Let me test: Injection screen currently... we're in Custom. Let me go Estradiol/Injection and type 10, then tap \"Vial\" and see if the value changes.\n\nActually, I realize I should be careful about spending too many steps. The whole test suite has other areas too. Let me time-box the carry-over investigation: a few more targeted tests.\n\nKey test: Estradiol/Tablet→Injection: is the field really empty? Yes, screenshot confirmed. Expected per changelog: \"every amount field is now carried over\". I'll consider it a bug candidate.\n\nBut maybe there's a subtlety: the amount field in Injection is only shown when \"Vial\" prep type is selected? It's shown with Ampule selected. Weird. Might indicate that the Injection form has *two* amount fields: one for Ampule and one for Vial, and only one is displayed. The label shown \"Vial strength (mg)\" with Ampule selected is suspicious. Maybe a UI bug: when Ampule is selected, the field should be \"Ampule strength (mg)\" or the amount should be hidden? Hmm.\n\nLet me examine: tap Vial and see what happens. Then switch back to Ampule. If the amount field label/value changes, that tells us there are two fields."}
