{"type": "thinking", "text": "Switching back to Vial restored 40 and 10. So the values are per-field retained. The \"10\" in Ampule came from...? Wait, when switching Vial→Ampule, Ampule's field showed 10. Ampule's field is a *different* field, but it showed 10, which was the Vial volume value. Hmm, could it be that the Ampule field and Vial volume field share the same state slot? Or maybe the \"Vial strength (mg)\" field was actually carrying over from the total amount?\n\nHmm, wait. Another explanation: when switching from Vial to Ampule, the form structure changed (2 fields → 1 field). The single remaining field might have been re-bound to the *second* field's state slot due to index shifting. That's a plausible bug: index-based state reuse causes the wrong value to appear.\n\nBut hold on: the changelog says \"Preparation form picker: every amount field is now carried over when the form is changed.\" Maybe the actual intended fix was exactly to carry all amount values across form changes, and the bug is that only one gets carried due to index collisions. Our observation: switching Vial (2 fields: 40, 10) → Ampule (1 field) shows 10 (the second value). Hmm, that suggests index 1 (volume) → index 0? Or perhaps the fields carry in display order but the first field (concentration 40) is lost. So \"every amount field\" not carried — one is lost, the other shown.\n\nAnd switching Ampule (1 field: 10) → Vial (2 fields): both 40 and 10 unchanged (retained), not overwritten. So no carry from Ampule.\n\nHmm, this is confusing. Let me think about what implementation could produce these observations:\n\nHypothesis: The form's amount fields have state keyed by *field ordinal* globally (not per form). There are N amount field slots (say 0..3) reused across forms. When you switch form, each field displays the slot corresponding to its position in the new form. When you asked for \"every amount field carried over\", maybe the app copies values from old positions to new positions.\n\nObservations:\n- Estradiol Tablet (slot 0 = 7) → Injection Ampule (slot 0 = \"Vial strength\") shows empty. Contradicts shared slot 0.\n- Gel (0.1, 80) → Tablet: 7 shows. Contradicts.\n- Vial (40,10) → Ampule: 10. \n\nAlternative hypothesis: Each form has its own state, and switching forms copies the *last* amount value from the old form to the FIRST amount field of the new form? Let's test:\n- Tablet (7) → Injection Ampule: old last = 7, new first = strength → should be 7, but empty. Contradicts.\n- Tablet (7) → Gel: old last = 7, new first = concentration → should be 7, but empty. Contradicts.\n\nHmm.\n\nWait, maybe state keyed by field id where the id is a fixed string like \"amount0\", \"amount1\". Different forms' fields with the same id share state:\n- Tablet strength uses amount0.\n- Capsule strength uses amount0 → Tablet↔Capsule carry (shared). ✓\n- Injection Ampule \"Vial strength\" uses, say, amount0 as well? Then Tablet 7 → Ampule should show 7. But it showed empty. ✗ (unless the field ids differ per category/form)\n- Gel concentration uses amount0, total gel uses amount1.\n- Gel (amount0=0.1, amount1=80) → Tablet (amount0) should show 0.1. But showed 7. ✗\n\nSo per-form per-field state.\n\nThen how did Vial volume 10 show in Ampule strength? Maybe Ampule's strength field state was never set, but initialization copies from... Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe the carry-over mechanism is: when you switch prep type within Injection, the app copies amount values from the old type's fields into the new type's fields *by index*, and if the new form has fewer fields, extra values are dropped. For Vial(40,10)→Ampule(1): the new field 0 should get 40. But it got 10. Unless the fields are ordered reversely or the \"10\" was from the second... Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe I mis-assigned: when switching Vial→Ampule, the displayed \"Vial strength (mg)\" = 10. But maybe the app copies the *last typed* value (10) rather than per-index. The last typed field was Vial volume = 10. That fits! And for Tablet(7)→Injection Ampule: last typed = 7, new field should be 7, but was empty. Contradicts.\n\nUnless... when switching injection form the app resets the amount because the medication list changed? Hmm.\n\nHold on, maybe I should re-verify that specific Tablet→Injection result because it's central. I did it twice: fresh sheet, typed 7 in Tablet, dismissed keyboard, tapped Injection → empty. That seems solid. Unless the Injection form's amount is stored per *medication*, and the medication was \"Estradiol valerate\" in both, so no reset.\n\nWait, actually — maybe the Injection form's first field (Ampule) is \"Vial strength (mg)\" and it's showing \"mg\" suffix but empty. Could it be that it actually holds the value but the text is not rendered due to a visual bug? The screenshot showed no text inside. So empty.\n\nOK here's another thought: maybe the carry-over only happens when the *number of amount fields is the same* (1→1) AND the field is \"strength\"; Tablet→Capsule are same category so they might share a medication \"strength\" model. Hmm.\n\nI think we need to be careful. Let's approach from the changelog's likely implementation: A typical Flutter app might implement the Add Medicine form with a `Map<String, String> _amounts` keyed by field key. On form change, it copies values from the previous form's amount fields to the new form's fields. The bug fix \"every amount field is now carried over\" suggests they iterate over all fields. The expected: if old form has 1 field with 7 and new form has 2 fields, both new fields get 7? Or the values carry by index?\n\nIf so, Tablet 7 → Gel: gel fields should be 7 and 7 (or 7 and empty). Observed empty/empty. That's clearly against expectation.\n\nUnless \"Preparation form picker\" refers to the \"Preparation type\" toggle (Ampule/Vial etc.). The UI label is \"Preparation type\" though. Hmm, but the task's changed note says \"Preparation form picker\" which matches the UI section \"Preparation form\". The \"Preparation type\" is a different picker.\n\nLet me test the cross-category behavior: The carry-over might be designed per category. Estradiol category forms share one amount state? For Estradiol:\n- Tablet: Tablet strength (mg)\n- Injection Ampule: Vial strength (mg)\n- Injection Vial: Concentration (mg/mL), Vial volume (mL)\n- Gel Container: Gel concentration (%), Total gel amount (g)\n- Gel Sachet: maybe different fields?\n- Patch Total dose: Total amount in patch (mg)\n- Patch Release rate: Patch release rate (μg/day)\n\nHmm, let me check Gel Sachet and see. Also Patch presets.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the changed note once more: \"Preparation form picker: every amount field is now carried over when the form is changed.\" Maybe the intended test scenario: enter values in ALL amount fields of one form, then change the form, then go back — all values are still there (\"carried over\" across the round trip). On this build, Gel (0.1, 80) ↔ Tablet: gel values were retained after returning (yes, we saw that). But Injection→Gel cleared them... Hmm, but that may have been because of a different form change.\n\nUgh, I'm going in circles. Let me run a precise controlled experiment to determine the actual behavior, then decide the bug.\n\nExperiment: Fresh sheet (Cancel → reopen). Estradiol.\n1. Tablet: type 7. → read.\n2. Switch Gel: read both fields.\n3. Type 0.3 (concentration), 80 (total).\n4. Switch Tablet: read.\n5. Switch Gel: read.\n6. Switch Injection (Ampule): read.\n7. Type 10 in strength.\n8. Switch Tablet: read.\n9. Switch Gel: read.\n\nLet me execute and carefully note. I'll use preset buttons where possible to avoid keyboard weirdness.\n\nActually the keyboard weirdness could be part of it: tapping another element while keyboard open might not commit the field text to state. E.g., in step 1→2 if the keyboard is open, the state might not have the typed value yet. In my Tablet→Injection test, I dismissed the keyboard first, so state should be committed.\n\nAnother important consideration: The `type_text` tool might not update the app state until a \"change\" event; it does since the field shows the text.\n\nHmm, OK. Let me now think about which bug to report. There's clearly some odd behavior with amount carry-over. But I ne", "truncated": true}
