{"type": "thinking", "text": "Now the Antiandrogen card shows two rows:\nRow 1: Spironolactone, \"Oral · 50 mg\", \"Last dose 2m ago\", \"Due 10:00 PM\"\nRow 2: Spironolactone, \"Oral · 2 tablets · 100 mg\", \"No logged dose yet\", \"Due 9:00 PM\"\n\nInteresting! The first row (group 1, 50 mg) shows \"Due 10:00 PM\"; the second row (group 2, 100 mg) shows \"Due 9:00 PM\". \n\nWait, it's 3:14 PM. The next due for group 1 is 10:00 PM (since its 3:12 PM logged record isn't counted). For group 2, the next due is 9:00 PM. So the card shows per-group rows with the next due time and dose. That's the \"dose looked up across all of the day's schedule slots\" — each row's dose and due time are found among the day's slots.\n\nHmm, so what's the potential bug? Maybe each row is per group and OK. But the changed note says \"the dose it shows is now looked up across all of the day's schedule slots\" — maybe the row's dose previously came from the medicine (50 mg) and now from the schedule (e.g., \"2 tablets · 100 mg\")? Interesting: Row 1 shows \"Oral · 50 mg\" while its group's medication row also shows \"Oral · 50 mg\". Row 2 shows \"Oral · 2 tablets · 100 mg\" matching group 2. So the dose is per-group schedule. Both correct.\n\nBut wait — there's possible inconsistency: Row 1 shows \"Last dose 2m ago\" and \"Due 10:00 PM\" — the logged 3:12 PM record was for the 10:00 PM slot, and it didn't count. So the due remains 10:00 PM. OK.\n\nNow, is there a bug? The card seems to be working. But let me test the ordering: Row 1 (Due 10PM) is listed before Row 2 (Due 9PM). Should the card list the next due first (9PM then 10PM)? Hmm, the note doesn't mention ordering.\n\nMaybe not a bug.\n\nHmm, let me think about the changed note again: \"Antiandrogen card on Home: the dose it shows is now looked up across all of the day's schedule slots.\"\n\nMaybe the card is supposed to show a *single* row per antiandrogen medicine with the dose from the day's schedule. Our card shows two rows because two schedule entries. Hmm, that seems intentional (each group). \n\nActually maybe the intended behavior: The card lists each antiandrogen *dose* for the day (like \"next doses\"). With 2 groups, two doses. OK.\n\nTo test the \"across all the day's schedule slots\" fix, consider: the antiandrogen is scheduled at multiple times in a group, and the group's next slot might be tomorrow (if all today's slots already passed/logged). Hmm.\n\nOr: the day's slots for a medicine could differ by day-of-week. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the card's \"Due\" should show the *next due time* found across all of the day's slots. Before logging, the card showed \"Due 10:00 PM\" for group 1 — group 1's slots today were 10PM and 11PM (12AM/8AM passed). So \"Due 10:00 PM\" is the first unfulfilled slot. After logging the 10PM slot early (not counted), due stayed 10PM. OK.\n\nWhat if I log the 10PM slot within its window? Then due should move to 11PM. The \"look up across all slots\" would find the next slot. Hmm, that's plausible: the bug would be that it only looked at the first slot and, after that one was fulfilled, showed nothing while another slot remained.\n\nI can't easily log within the window (times are 10PM, current 3PM). I could change the device clock? Not possible. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, create a group with slots in the past today, then log the earliest one within its window? The fulfillment window is ±? The \"Outside fulfillment window\" warning appeared at -6h. What's the window? Maybe ±4 hours? If I set a slot at 1:00 PM (past), logging now at 3:14 PM would be -2h14m, likely within the window (±2h?). Then the \"0/N\" would increment. Then if the group has a later slot, the card should show that next slot.\n\nHmm. This is getting long. Let me instead focus on the clear prep-form issue and look for other bugs in the group schedule UI.\n\nActually, wait. There’s a potentially important bug in the group editor times: When the group is locked (related records), the times section showed \"TIMES OF DAY · 4\" and only \"12:00 AM\" visible; but that's scroll cut-off.\n\nLet me check the \"Weekly\" schedule? Not in the changed notes.\n\nLet me re-examine the ordering feature more: the times are sorted in the editor. What about the Plan group row order of times \"12:00 AM/8:00 AM/10:00 PM/11:00 PM\" — sorted. Good.\n\nPotential bug: adding a time while another is being edited? Or duplicate times? Let me test duplicates: add a time equal to an existing one. Does it get removed/merged? Not required.\n\nPotential bug: The Times of Day section count \"· 4\" and the list. Looks fine.\n\nLet me test the 12-hour AM/PM ordering with 12:00 PM (noon) and 1:00 PM, since 12 PM should sort after 11 AM. Our sort worked for 12 AM first. Let me test adding 12:00 PM to group 2 (editable? group 2 has no records yet, so editable). Hmm, that's more data.\n\nActually, the group editor ordering passed two cases. Good enough.\n\nNow let me look for other bugs in the Home cards. The Estradiol card: \"0 pg/mL\", \"Below\", \"No change from 24h ago\", \"No logged dose yet\". With a logged estradiol dose, the estimate stays 0? That's suspicious: I logged spironolactone, not estradiol. Estradiol has no logged dose, so 0 is expected. But the 7-day chart shows a projection curve of estradiol levels (150). Interesting — the chart seems to show a *forecast* from schedule? Because no estradiol doses logged, yet the chart shows levels rising. Hmm, maybe the chart shows \"population estimate\" from the schedule (2mg). That's plausible.\n\nWait, \"Estradiol · population estimate\" 0 pg/mL with \"No logged dose yet\", but the 7-day trend shows a curve up to ~150? Hmm, in the initial fresh-install state, the chart was flat at 0. Now after creating a schedule with estradiol, the chart shows a projection. Hmm, odd: with no logged dose, the estimate is 0 but forecast shows levels. Maybe the chart shows the estimate based on the schedule projections. Not sure.\n\nCould this be inconsistency? The card says 0 pg/mL while the chart shows 100-150 pg/mL at the right edge. Let me look: the chart's y-axis was initially 0..2 (weird units), then after adding schedule it became 0..200 with a curve reaching ~100 by 11/17. The current value is 0 (today, 11/14) but the curve at today is ~100? Let me look at the screenshot: the vertical line at \"S\" (today) is at the 0 level, and the curve starts from S and rises after. So today = 0, future = projected. That's consistent: from today, projected levels rise.\n\nHmm OK.\n\nLet me look at the antiandrogen card again: the two rows are both \"Spironolactone\" with correct doses. One thing to check: after editing/deleting the logged record, does the card update? Let me test the record edit path: tap the \"-6h\" row (element 16) to edit the record; maybe delete it. That tests reversal/consistency.\n\nActually, we might have found a bug in the antiandrogen card: it shows the SAME medicine twice, which could be intentional (two groups). Hmm.\n\nLet me think about the \"dose lookup across all of the day's schedule slots\" bug possibility differently. Suppose the card shows one row per antiandrogen medicine (not per group). With two groups, we see two rows of the same medicine. That could actually be the bug! If the card is supposed to show one row per *medicine* with the next dose across all its slots, then showing duplicate rows is wrong. But the note says \"the dose it shows is now looked up across all of the day's schedule slots\" — implying the card shows ONE dose for the medicine, looked up across slots. With two groups, the expected might be a single row showing... hmm, which dose? Unclear.\n\nWait, maybe the antiandrogen card is per *group* in the UI (\"Antiandrogen\" section listing active antiandrogen meds). The two rows correspond to two groups, each with \"Due\" times. That seems like a deliberate design: \"Here are your antiandrogen meds and when they're due.\"\n\nHmm.\n\nLet me test one more scenario for the Home card: What if the antiandrogen medicine is in a group but the group's next slot is *tomorrow* (all today's slots passed). Does the card show a dose at all? The \"dose looked up across all of the day's schedule slots\" might then fail (showing something wrong). I could create a third group with spironolactone at 6:00 AM (past today), so no slots ", "truncated": true}
