{"type": "thinking", "text": "55 was retained. So the injection field state persists across form changes even with keyboard open. So per-form retention is solid.\n\nConclusion: The prep form state retention works. The changelog item is probably satisfied (assuming \"carried over\" = retained across form changes). Hmm. But my earlier observation of Tablet→Injection empty is consistent: Injection had never been filled; nothing to carry *into* it. If the changelog meant \"values are copied from the old form to the new form\", it's not implemented. But the phrase \"every amount field is now carried over when the form is changed\" strongly suggests copying to the new form. Argh.\n\nLet me test with the *specific* scenario the changelog might describe: When changing the form, the amount fields of the OLD form should be carried into the new form. Since that's not happening (Tablet 2 → Gel empty), maybe it IS a bug. But the per-form retention also matches \"values carried over\" loosely.\n\nHmm, let me look at it from the \"changed in this build\" testing standpoint: The QA should verify the changed behavior. If the build's behavior doesn't match the changelog, it's a bug. The changelog says \"every amount field is now carried over when the form is changed.\" The natural user-facing expectation: switch from Tablet (strength 2 mg) to Injection → the injection amount field shows 2 mg. It doesn't. So the fix is incomplete → bug.\n\nBut the counter-evidence: Tablet→Capsule DOES carry. So the feature exists but is form-specific. Maybe the intended fix was only for fields that exist in both forms (same semantic). For Tablet→Capsule, both have strength mg. For Tablet→Injection Ampule, \"Vial strength (mg)\" — same unit/semantic! So it should carry. It doesn't → bug.\n\nWait, maybe the carry happens only when the *medication* remains the same? Tablet (Estradiol valerate) → Capsule (Dutasteride, auto-changed) — medication changed, still carried. So no.\n\nHmm, maybe the carry happens only when the form *picker layout* changes to a form with the same amount field count (1→1). Injection Ampule has 1 field. So Tablet→Ampule should carry. It didn't. Unless the Injection form's field is `vialStrength` while Tablet's is `tabletStrength` and Capsule's is... maybe both Tablet and Capsule use the same `strength` key while injection uses `vialStrength` and gel uses `concentration`/`totalAmount`.\n\nIf the implementation copies by matching keys, then only same-key fields carry. The changelog \"every amount field\" would then mean: for forms that share keys, all fields carry. But Tablet/Capsule are the only pair sharing a key. Hmm, that's a weird fix.\n\nAlternatively, the implementation might copy by *index*, and the reason Tablet→Capsule works is coincidence: index 0 of the old form's amount list = 2 → index 0 of new. Then Tablet→Injection index 0 = 2 should also work. It doesn't. So not index-based either.\n\nUnless the Injection form's amount field is an *optional* field that only appears when Vial selected, but the label is \"Vial strength (mg)\" under Ampule. Wait — maybe the \"Vial strength (mg)\" field under Ampule mode is NOT an amount field for the Ampule; maybe it's a leftover *hidden* field for vial strength that is displayed erroneously. And the real Ampule amount field might be something else (missing!). Hmm! Look: under Ampule, the field label says \"Vial strength (mg)\". That IS odd. Maybe the Ampule form *should* have an \"Ampule strength (mg)\" field, but the app is showing the Vial field (bug). Then the \"carry-over\" of Tablet strength wouldn't apply because the ampule's own amount is missing.\n\nHmm, but the preset doses 5 mg and 10 mg suggest ampule doses. The label \"Vial strength (mg)\" may just be generic.\n\nI'm going too deep. Let me consider the third changelog item and check it more (ordering works). And Home card (seems to work). Then decide on filing the carry-over bug.\n\nActually, let me try the most direct test of the changelog claim: \"every amount field is now carried over when the form is changed.\"\n\nFor the Claim to be true, if I enter values in form A's amount fields and change to form B, form B's amount fields must show those values. We tested:\n- Tablet(2) → Gel: empty. FAIL.\n- Tablet(2) → Injection: empty. FAIL.\n- Tablet(2) → Capsule: 2. PASS (same field semantic).\n- Vial(40,10) → Ampule: 10 (not 40). Partial.\n\nSo the claim fails for most form changes. This is a legitimate bug report: \"Changing the preparation form does not carry over the entered amount(s); the new form's amount fields are empty.\"\n\nBut wait — maybe the QA intent of the changed note is the opposite: the fix ensures each form's amount isn't lost when switching *back*. In that case our test passes. Ugh.\n\nLet me look for wording in the app: Maybe there's a hint/description. The \"Preset doses are reference values...\" no.\n\nAlternative: Check if there is a difference between \"Preparation form\" and \"Preparation type\" in the UI. The Add medicine sheet has \"Preparation form\" (Tablet/Injection/Gel/Patch) and \"Preparation type\" (Ampule/Vial; Container/Sachet; Total dose/Release rate). The changelog says \"Preparation form picker\". So it's the outer picker. For the outer picker, values are retained per form but not copied. Hmm. If the intended fix was copying, the copy should happen on the outer picker. It doesn't.\n\nOK, I'll treat \"not copied across forms\" as a candidate bug but keep testing to see if there are other more clear-cut bugs. Actually, hold on. Let me check the actual behavior when switching forms *without ever entering* in the target: Tablet 2 → Injection shows empty. Yes, that's the copy failure.\n\nLet me look at the *second* changed item and see if the \"group editor schedule ordering\" has a subtle bug: the Plan group row showed \"12:00 AM/8:00 AM/10:00 PM/11:00 PM\" (sorted). The editor showed sorted. What about when editing an existing time to be earlier/later — we tested (11PM sorted correctly). What about adding a time in \"Weekly\" schedule? Not needed.\n\nWhat about duplicates? If I add the same time twice, does the section show both? That's allowed probably.\n\nWhat about \"Night owl mode\" affecting ordering? With night owl mode, times before 4:00 AM count as previous day. The chronological order in the section — would a 2:00 AM time be sorted first or last? Chronologically within the \"day\" for night owls, 2:00 AM would be at the end (after 11 PM). The changelog says \"reminder times in the section are now ordered chronologically.\" Hmm — with night-owl mode ON, is 2:00 AM considered the end of the day? The app says \"Doses before 4:00 AM count as the previous day.\" So a night-owl user's chronological order might place 2:00 AM after 11:00 PM. If the fix orders strictly by clock time (12AM first), 2AM would appear first, which would be wrong for night owls. That could be the introduced bug! Hmm, but the changelog says \"ordered chronologically\" — the bug may be that it sorts by 12-hour string or something.\n\nOur tests: 12:00 AM sorted first, 8 AM, 10 PM, 11 PM — proper clock order. That's likely the intended fix. Night owl mode is an edge case; maybe the section should respect night owl's day boundary? The description says \"Doses before 4:00 AM count as the previous day\" — that's about which day the dose belongs to, not the ordering within the section. The section might still sort by clock time. Unclear. I could test with night owl on: add 2:00 AM and see where it sorts. If the expected is unknown, don't report.\n\nLet me now test the remaining Home card areas more broadly (the Home tab cards). The Home has:\n- Estradiol population estimate card\n- 7-day trend chart\n- Antiandrogen card\n- TODAY list\n- UPCOMING list\n\nThe \"Home tab cards\" area includes these. Let me test the antiandrogen card more (it's the changed one) and look for bugs like:\n- Dose displayed incorrectly after changing the schedule / after logging.\n- Duplicate cards.\n- State persistence across tabs.\n\nOne thing I noticed: the antiandrogen card row shows \"Due 10:00 PM\" for the group-1 row even though the 10PM dose was logged (early, not counted). Fine.\n\nAnother: The two rows both belong to the same \"Antiandrogen\" card and both are Sp", "truncated": true}
